I reviewed only commit c136e9eb (`feat(feat-lazy-bacteria-game): support Bacteria Wars with tips and ads`). Your local `main` is behind, so `main...feat/bacteria-wars-support` also picks up the #2570 CDK commit and release commits that are already merged; I left those out. Nothing was verified, as the recipe asked: these are candidate findings, not confirmed bugs.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "libs/features/lazy/bacteria-game/src/lib/support/twa-session.ts",
    "line": 10,
    "summary": "The game decides it is inside the Play Store app when the referrer is any `android-app://` value, not only `android-app://de.wolsok.bacteriawars`, and keeps that flag for the whole session.",
    "failure_scenario": "A web player opens bacteria-wars.wolsok.de from the Google app or Gmail in Chrome, so the referrer is `android-app://com.google.android.googlequicksearchbox/`. The game treats the visit as the app: `url()` is null, so the Buy Me a Coffee link is hidden. Chrome has no Digital Goods bridge there, so no tip products load and the player gets no way to support the game at all, for the rest of the session (sessionStorage flag)."
  },
  {
    "file": "apps/bacteria-wars-android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml",
    "line": 48,
    "summary": "With `FALLBACK_STRATEGY=webview`, or a TWA browser that sends no `android-app://` referrer, `isTwaSession` returns false inside the Play app, so the game shows the Buy Me a Coffee link there.",
    "failure_scenario": "On a phone without a TWA-capable Chrome, the app falls back to WebView, which loads the launch URL with no referrer. The game then counts as 'web', and the main menu and How to play show an external donation link inside a Play-distributed app, which breaks Play's payments policy and risks removal. Root-cause fix: tag the launch URL in build.gradle.kts (e.g. `?source=twa`) and detect the app from that, not from the referrer."
  },
  {
    "file": "libs/features/lazy/bacteria-game/src/lib/support/game-support.service.ts",
    "line": 81,
    "summary": "`consumePending` runs once, fire-and-forget, at the same moment as the first `loadProducts`, and is never retried, even though the loop below exists because the billing bridge is often not bound yet at start.",
    "failure_scenario": "Last session the app closed between payment and consume, so a `tip_small` purchase is still unconsumed. At the next start `digitalGoods()` resolves null (bridge not bound), so `consumePending` returns early; `loadProducts` then succeeds on retry 2. The purchase stays unconsumed: tapping `tip_small` again fails with 'already owned' and shows the 'did not go through' notice. If this repeats for 3 days, Play refunds the tip because it was never acknowledged."
  },
  {
    "file": "libs/features/lazy/bacteria-game/src/lib/support/game-ads.service.ts",
    "line": 76,
    "summary": "Ads show only after a TCF callback arrives. If `__tcfapi` never appears within the 30 s poll, `answered` stays false for the rest of the session and there is no fallback.",
    "failure_scenario": "A Privacy & Messaging message that is not published, a region where Google's CMP does not install the TCF API, or a slow network that loads the ad script after 30 s: `ready()` stays false, so `<feat-lazy-bac-game-ad-slot>` never renders an ad, with no error and nothing logged."
  },
  {
    "file": "libs/features/lazy/bacteria-game/src/lib/support/game-ads.service.ts",
    "line": 93,
    "summary": "`openConsentSettings` captures the `googlefc` object at push time and calls `googlefc.showRevocationMessage` on that copy, not on `window.googlefc` at callback time as Google's snippet does. The How to play button also appears whenever ads are configured, even with no CMP loaded.",
    "failure_scenario": "If the Funding Choices script replaces `window.googlefc` instead of extending the stub, the queued callback calls the method on the stale object, which does nothing, so 'Ad privacy settings' silently fails. The same happens when an ad blocker stops the CMP, and players cannot withdraw consent as the privacy policy (section 7) promises."
  },
  {
    "file": "libs/features/lazy/bacteria-game/src/lib/menu/main-menu.component.scss",
    "line": 80,
    "summary": "The tiles changed from 3 fixed columns to `grid-auto-flow: column`, so the Support tile puts 4 tiles in one row, and the landscape media query (`max-height: 30rem`) also lays each tile out as a row next to a 16rem card.",
    "failure_scenario": "On a phone held sideways (about 740 px wide, minus the 16rem card), each tile is about 100 px wide holding a 24 px icon plus 'How to play' side by side. Labels wrap or overflow; on 320 px portrait phones the four tiles get cramped too."
  },
  {
    "file": "libs/features/lazy/bacteria-game/src/lib/support/ad-slot.component.ts",
    "line": 28,
    "summary": "The `personalised` signal and the `data-npa` attribute duplicate what AdSense already reads from the TCF string, and `data-npa` is not a documented AdSense `<ins>` attribute (AdSense uses `requestNonPersonalizedAds` on the queue).",
    "failure_scenario": "This is extra state and binding that likely has no effect. A maintainer may believe the attribute enforces non-personalised ads, but the ad served is decided by the CMP's TC string, so the purpose/vendor math in `apply()` is maintenance cost with no effect."
  },
  {
    "file": "libs/features/lazy/bacteria-game/src/lib/support/game-ads.service.ts",
    "line": 58,
    "summary": "The public `consentAnswered` signal is dead code: nothing in libs/ or apps/ reads it, because `ready` already covers it.",
    "failure_scenario": "It is dead public surface on a root service, which grows the API and misleads readers about what consumers rely on."
  },
  {
    "file": "apps/bacteria-game-remote/src/environments/environment.ts",
    "line": 13,
    "summary": "The same `support` block (handle 'wolf' and the three tip ids) is copy-pasted into environment.ts, environment.emulator.ts and environment.prod.ts.",
    "failure_scenario": "Adding or renaming a Play product (e.g. `tip_huge`) means editing three files. Missing one makes dev and prod offer different tips, and the README only points at environment.prod.ts. A shared constant spread with `ads` overridden per file avoids this."
  },
  {
    "file": "libs/features/lazy/bacteria-game/src/lib/support/game-ads.service.spec.ts",
    "line": 49,
    "summary": "Tests call `start()` under real timers without a CMP, which leaves `setInterval` polls running for up to 30 s after the test. `start()` itself is not idempotent for the poll or the TCF listener.",
    "failure_scenario": "'loads the ad script once' calls `start()` twice, which starts two real 200 ms intervals that keep polling into later tests. Once a later test runs `installCmp()`, a stale interval can register its own callback and overwrite `listener`, which makes tests flaky and keeps the Jest worker alive. In production, a second `start()` would register a second TCF listener."
  }
]
```
